{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: duplicated `requiredContexts` parse block — dead copy of the lines just above**

This hunk adds a second, verbatim copy of the `rcContexts` parse block. Lines 2046–2053 repeat lines 2030–2037 exactly (the pre-existing block was kept as context, then the same parse was re-added below the new gh-755 comment):

```js
var rcAny = rule.anyCancelled === true;
var rcContexts = [];                                    // ← duplicate
if (Array.isArray(rule.requiredContexts)) {             // ← duplicate
    rcContexts = rule.requiredContexts;
} else if (typeof rule.requiredContexts === 'string' && rule.requiredContexts) {
    rcContexts = rule.requiredContexts.split(',').map(function (s) {
        return s.trim();
    }).filter(Boolean);
}
```

`var` re-declaration makes this functionally harmless today (the second block overwrites the first with identical results), but it is exactly the copy-paste drift this PR's own comments warn against ("a drifted copy here would let the fail path arm rework on rollups computePrStatus itself never calls red"). If someone later edits one copy (e.g. adds CSV-quote handling or a trim rule), the two silently diverge.

Delete the first block (or the second — keep one) so the parse exists once, directly above the `rcAny` check:

```js
var rcContexts = [];
if (Array.isArray(rule.requiredContexts)) {
    rcContexts = rule.requiredContexts;
} else if (typeof rule.requiredContexts === 'string' && rule.requiredContexts) {
    rcContexts = rule.requiredContexts.split(',').map(function (s) {
        return s.trim();
    }).filter(Boolean);
}
// gh-755: rule.anyCancelled switches to the ANY-cancelled mode …
var rcAny = rule.anyCancelled === true;
```

Related nit in the same region: when a rule carries **both** `anyCancelled: true` and `requiredContexts`, the name list is silently ignored (deliberate per the tests, but invisible to an operator reading the deployed JSON). A one-line `console.warn` when `rcAny && rcContexts.length` would make the misconfiguration visible.
"}

